.TH TEE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
tee \- copy standard input to standard output and to files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tee
.RB [ \-ai ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tee
copies standard input to standard output and to each
.IR file ,
so that a pipeline can be saved to files and still be passed on.
It reads standard input to end of file, in blocks.
It writes each block to standard output, then to each
.I file
in command-line order, before it reads the next block.
Nothing is buffered, so every destination has a block as soon as it is
read.
The bytes are copied unchanged, except for output to a console, as
described in
.BR NOTES .
With no
.I file
operands,
.B tee
copies standard input to standard output.
.PP
Each
.I file
is created if it does not exist, and is truncated unless
.B \-a
is given.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
other destinations still receive the data.
.PP
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is standard output again, not a file, so the data is written to standard
output a second time.
To name a file called
.BR \- ,
write
.BR ./\- .
.PP
A
.I file
named twice is opened twice.
Without
.BR \-a ,
both streams write from the start of the file, so it ends up holding one
copy of the input.
With
.BR \-a ,
it holds two.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ai ,
and may follow
.I file
operands.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so that a
.I file
can begin with
.BR \- .
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help .
An invalid option prints an error and a usage message on standard error,
and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-a
Append to each
.I file
instead of truncating it.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore SIGINT, the interrupt signal that Ctrl-C raises.
.B tee
still ends on Ctrl-Break or
.BR "kill \-INT" .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-a
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
A file mode creation mask in octal.
When it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
a file that
.B tee
creates is read-only, although
.B tee
still writes to it.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a
.I file
could not be opened, standard input could not be read, or a write to a
.I file
failed.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output, or a
.IR file ,
is a pipe that was closed before all the input was copied.
.B tee
exits at once, without a message, and copies no further input to any
destination.
.PP
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tee: \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and skipped.
A failed write to a
.IR file ,
other than to a closed pipe, does not stop
.BR tee ,
which keeps copying to every destination.
It is noticed only when the input ends, and is then reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tee: \fIfile\fR: I/O error
.EE
.RE
.PP
for the first failing
.I file
only.
A failed write to standard output, other than to a closed pipe, is not
reported and does not affect the exit status, unless a
.I file
operand is
.BR \- .
A read error on standard input ends the input without a message.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save the output of a build and watch it at the same time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | tee build.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a line to a log and show it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo "build started" | tee \-a build.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a sorted copy of a list while counting its lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort names.txt | tee sorted.txt | wc \-l
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the middle of a pipeline on the terminal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls | tee /dev/stderr | wc \-l
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep logging a program's output after Ctrl-C interrupts it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ./stress\-test | tee \-i stress.log
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR tee :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
and no
.BR \-p .
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is standard output, where GNU
.B tee
creates a file of that name.
.IP \(bu 2
A failed write is reported only when the input ends, and
.B tee
keeps writing to that
.IR file .
.PP
Standard input and output and the files are binary, so CR LF line
endings and Ctrl-Z characters are copied unchanged.
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, non-ASCII characters typed at a
console arrive in the console's input code page, such as 437, not in
UTF-8.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
.PP
File names may use drive letters, backslashes, and forward slashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
.I /dev/tty
(the console), and
.I /dev/fd/N
work as on Unix.
A directory, or a file with the read-only attribute, fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
When standard output, or a file such as
.IR /dev/tty ,
is a console, text is converted for display from the ANSI code page,
which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, unless the first
of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
that is set is
.BR C .
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
A block written to a console is cut at its first NUL byte, and the rest
of the block is not shown.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR sh (1)
